NAME
    basic - the BASIC interpreter

SYNOPSIS
    basic
    basic file
    basic -r file

DESCRIPTION
    Y1 BASIC: 16-bit integers, strings, and programs kept in files.
    basic alone shows the prompt "> ". basic file LOADs the file
    first; basic -r file LOADs it, RUNs it and returns to the shell,
    so a BASIC program can be a command or a line of a script. BYE,
    or Ctrl-D at an empty prompt, returns to the shell.

    A line that starts with a number is stored in the program (the
    same number again replaces the line, the number alone deletes it);
    a line without one runs at once. Keywords may be typed in any
    case; LIST shows them in capitals. Several statements go on one
    line separated by ":".

    Statements: PRINT (";" joins, "," puts a space, a trailing ; or ,
    keeps the line open), LET (optional), IF expr THEN statement or
    line number, FOR v = a TO b [STEP s] ... NEXT [v], GOTO n,
    GOSUB n ... RETURN, INPUT ["prompt";] v or v$, POKE addr, byte,
    REM, END.
    Commands: RUN, LIST, NEW, SAVE "file", LOAD "file", HELP, BYE.
    Files: OPEN "file" FOR OUTPUT or FOR INPUT, PRINT# value, INPUT#
    v or v$ (one value per line), EOF(1) (1 at the end of the file),
    CLOSE. One file is open at a time; RUN and the end of a program
    close it.
    Functions: ABS(x) RND(n) (1..n) PEEK(addr) LEN(s$) ASC(s$)
    VAL(s$) CHR$(n) LEFT$(s$,n) RIGHT$(s$,n) MID$(s$,start[,n])
    STR$(n).
    Operators: + - * / % (remainder), = <> < > <= >= (1 true, 0
    false; numbers compare signed, strings by character code), and
    + joins strings. Numbers are written in decimal or as 0x1F.

    Graphics, on the video card's graphics layer: 640 x 400 dots,
    x 0..639 from the left, y 0..399 from the top, each dot on or off.
        SCREEN n       what is shown: 0 the text alone, 1 the text
                       and the graphics, 2 the graphics alone, 3 the
                       two XORed (text reads dark on a filled area).
                       The first SCREEN after a reset also starts the
                       video card, as video init does
        CLS            every dot off (the text stays)
        COLOR c        the ink of what is drawn next: 1 draws (as at
                       the start), 0 erases, 2 inverts
        PIXELW x,y     one dot
        LINE x0,y0,x1,y1
        BOX x0,y0,x1,y1[,FILL]   a rectangle by two opposite corners,
                       its outline, or filled
        CIRCLE x,y,r[,FILL]      a circle, its outline, or filled
        CIRCLE x,y,rx,ry[,FILL]  an ellipse: rx to each side of the
                       middle, ry above and below it. A radius is 0
                       to 511 (0: a line, or a dot)
        GTEXT x,y,size,a$   text as dots, x,y its top left corner.
                       The characters are 5 x 7 dots in a cell of
                       6 x 8, each dot size x size (size 1 to 63):
                       106 characters across at size 1, 53 at size 2.
                       Only the letters' own dots are drawn, in the
                       COLOR's ink; a$ is any string expression
        PAGE draw,show the card has two pages, 0 and 1: the one the
                       statements draw on (and PIXELR reads, and CLS
                       clears) and the one on the screen. Draw on the
                       hidden page, then show it: PAGE 1,0 ... PAGE 0,1
        PAINT x,y      fills the region the dot x,y is in: the dots
                       joined to it - up, down, left, right - that
                       are as it is (off, or on) all become what the
                       COLOR makes of it: on with 1, off with 0, the
                       other with 2. So PAINT fills an outline's
                       inside, COLOR 0 : PAINT takes a filled shape
                       away. Nothing if the dot is that already. A
                       dot takes three times as long as one of a
                       filled BOX: 100 x 100 dots about two minutes.
                       PAINT remembers up to 128 places it must come
                       back to; a region of very many separate arms
                       needs more and stops partly filled:
                       ?PAINT TOO DEEP
        PIXELR(x,y)    a function: 1 if the dot is on, else 0
    What is off the screen is not drawn: a shape may hang over an
    edge. Draw with SCREEN 0 and show the picture afterwards, or with
    SCREEN 1 and watch. The names of these statements, and FILL, can
    no longer be variables.
    A statement runs to its end: Ctrl-C is looked at between the
    lines of a program. A filled BOX of the whole screen takes about
    a quarter of an hour and a PAINT of it three quarters.

    SAVE writes the program as text, exactly as LIST shows it, so a
    saved program can be read with cat, changed with vi and LOADed
    back. LOAD replaces the program in memory with the file's lines.

    Ctrl-C stops a running program with "BREAK IN n". Any other key
    typed while a program runs is kept for its next INPUT or the next
    prompt, but a key typed while basic -r runs is lost when the
    program ends.

ERRORS
    ?SYNTAX ERROR [IN n]   the line cannot be read; when typed, a
                           line with unbalanced () or "" or one that
                           starts with THEN, TO, a function... is
                           refused and not stored
    ?UNDEF'D LINE n IN m   GOTO or GOSUB to a line that is not there
    ?ONLY IN A PROGRAM     GOTO, GOSUB, RETURN or NEXT typed at the
                           prompt
    ?RETURN WITHOUT GOSUB, ?NEXT WITHOUT FOR, ?GOSUB TOO DEEP,
    ?FOR TOO DEEP, ?TOO MANY VARIABLES, ?TOO MANY STRINGS,
    ?OUT OF MEMORY, ?NO FILE, ?CANNOT SAVE, ?CANNOT OPEN

LIMITS
    Numbers -32768..32767 (whole numbers only; / rounds toward 0, a
    division by 0 gives 0). Names: 6 significant letters or digits,
    32 number variables and 16 strings of up to 32 characters. FOR and
    GOSUB nest 8 deep. Program lines up to 98 characters; 12286 bytes
    of program (basicc, the reference in C: 4102). No arrays.

EXAMPLES
    > 10 for i = 1 to 3 : print i, i*i : next i
    > run
    > save "squares.bas"
    > bye
    /> basic -r squares.bas

    > 10 screen 1 : cls : box 0,0,639,399
    > 20 for i = 0 to 390 step 30 : line 0,i,639,399-i : next i
    > 30 circle 320,200,100 : box 280,180,360,220,fill
    > 40 circle 100,100,30 : paint 100,100
    > run

NOTES
    Ported from P8X basic/basic.c 2026-11-09, without its graphics
    statements. New: programs saved as text, basic -r, INPUT with a
    prompt, Ctrl-C, deeper FOR and GOSUB. Graphics for this
    machine's video card V4 since 2026-11-20 (os/lib_gfx.c); a
    line is one dot wide. basicc, the reference this BASIC is
    held to, has a program area of 4102 bytes: its room goes to
    its code.
    Written in C (os/commands/basic.c, now basicc, the specification)
    and 2026-11-11 again in YACC1 assembly
    (os/commands-asm/basic.asm): this basic, which behaves exactly as
    basicc and needs about a sixth of its instructions for a line.
    The ROM has its own, smaller BASIC (the monitor's I command); it
    cannot load or save programs.

SEE ALSO
    basicc, vi, cat, sh
